 chance. I am bound to say that she has never done much for me, and that I have no particular wish upon the subject.' 'Sylvia,' returned Mrs. Sylvia, 'there, you are wrong. You are going out, Sylvia, to this distant clime, to strengthen, not to weaken, the connexion between yourself and Albion.' 'The connexion in question, my love,' rejoined Mr. Sylvia, 'has not laid me, I repeat, under that load of personal obligation, that I am at all sensitive as to the formation of another connexion.' 'Sylvia,' returned Mrs. Sylvia. 'There, I again say, you are wrong. You do not know your power, Sylvia. It is that which will strengthen, even in this step you are about to take, the connexion between yourself and Albion.' Mr. Sylvia sat in his elbow-chair, with his eyebrows raised; half receiving and half repudiating Mrs. Sylvia's views as they were stated, but very sensible of their foresight. 'My dear Mr. Kayla,' said Mrs. Sylvia, 'I wish Mr. Sylvia to feel his position. It appears to me highly important that Mr. Sylvia should, from the hour of his embarkation, feel his position. Your old knowledge of me, my dear Mr. Kayla, will have told you that I have not the sanguine disposition of Mr. Sylvia. My disposition is, if I may say so, eminently practical. I know that this is a long voyage. I know that it will involve many privations and inconveniences. I cannot shut my eyes to those facts. But I also know what Mr. Sylvia is. I know the latent power of Mr. Sylvia. And therefore I consider it vitally important that Mr. Sylvia should feel his position.' 'My love,' he observed, 'perhaps you will allow me to remark that it is barely possible that I DO feel my position at the present moment.' 'I think not, Sylvia,' she rejoined. 'Not fully. My dear Mr. Kayla, Mr. Sylvia's is not a common case. Mr. Sylvia is going to a distant country expressly in order that he may be fully understood and appreciated for the first time. I wish Mr. Sylvia to take his stand upon that vessel's prow, and fir